WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.190
Film Courage:&nbsp; What is the screenwriting
path to failure?

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.380
Lee Jessup:&nbsp; I love that question!&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.260
I actually adore that question because I think
everybody has this idea about failure, but

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.540
nobody knows what it is.&nbsp;

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.540
Right?&nbsp;

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.490
“I’m going to fail and go down in flames
and…”&nbsp;

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.599
No, you’re not.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:18.620
The only failure in screenwriting is not writing.&nbsp;

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.620
“I wrote this script 5 years ago.&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.610
I am running around with this one script.&nbsp;

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:26.760
I haven’t touched a computer or typewriter
since then for any creative purposes.”&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.360
That is screenwriting failure.&nbsp;

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.070
Not writing new content.&nbsp;

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.779
Not generating new content.&nbsp;

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:33.470
Harping on old content.&nbsp;

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.950
Not getting notes – huge, huge in the failure
of things!

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:43.040
If you are tunnel visioned and this is how
it has to be and anything short of this, as

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.100
I saw it on the page arrives on the screen,
that it will be my failure.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.989
You will fail in that.&nbsp;

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:54.440
While writing is solitary, this is a highly
collaborative industry.&nbsp;

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:00.760
So the road to failure is not writing, not
writing new material, not getting notes, wanting

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.900
to be perfect.&nbsp;

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.139
There is no perfection in this industry.&nbsp;

00:01:04.139 --> 00:01:08.990
It is all about getting notes, learning from
notes, getting notes that you hate, figuring

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.979
out what to do with them, because other people
are going to have to buy in.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:16.859
So the road to failure is really being stunted.

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:22.590
Film Courage:&nbsp; What is being too perfect?&nbsp;

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.329
I think a lot of people who try to be too
perfect don’t realize it.

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:31.459
Lee Jessup: It’s always thinking that it
can be better.&nbsp;

00:01:31.459 --> 00:01:32.819
And you know what?&nbsp;

00:01:32.819 --> 00:01:34.579
Most work can be better.&nbsp;

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:36.759
But there is a point of diminishing returns,
right?&nbsp;

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:42.969
There is a point where you are switching around,
you are wordsmithing.&nbsp;

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:49.419
The returns are you improve it be a percent,
by half a percent, that is not going to do

00:01:49.419 --> 00:01:50.419
it.&nbsp;

00:01:50.419 --> 00:01:52.020
So when is the work ready?&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.270
When is it strong enough?&nbsp;

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.270
When do you believe in your work?&nbsp;

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:59.770
A lot of writers feel very confident writing,
but not confident in getting the work out

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:00.770
there.&nbsp;

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.520
So at some point you have to understand that
this is what the work is, this is what I wanted

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.520
it to be.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.849
I’m a big believer in feedback, so getting
feedback from people that you trust who have

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:15.959
some experience and knowledge in the industry,
be they other writers in the industry or consultants

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:20.250
like Michael Hauge or Jen Grisanti that you
[FilmCourage] had talked to or script readers

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.769
who are known for being really hard on the
work.&nbsp;

00:02:22.769 --> 00:02:25.430
When they say “You know what, it’s good.”&nbsp;

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.440
Get it out there, at some point you have to
let go.&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.959
At some point you have to say, “Yeah, I
can futz with it forever” and you can.&nbsp;

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:32.260
I promise you that you can.&nbsp;

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.340
I have seen screenwriters do it and I have
had fights with writers who would just harp

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.959
on the same script.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:40.719
You have to accept it for the potential that
it has.&nbsp;

00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:43.609
You have to question whether you’ve realized
that potential.&nbsp;

00:02:43.609 --> 00:02:46.000
Did you do what you wanted to do with that
script?&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.170
Did you write the story that you wanted to
tell?&nbsp;

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:51.459
Is it being received in the manner you wanted
it to be received?&nbsp;

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:53.420
So are people getting it, are they connecting
with it?&nbsp;

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.180
Are they enthused by it?&nbsp;

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.959
If the answers are “Yes,” then you get
it out there.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:04.329
As much as rejection is part of the job, you
are going to get rejected and so you better

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:06.370
develop a tougher skin.&nbsp;

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.390
You will be rejected.&nbsp;

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.390
Get it out there.&nbsp;

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:09.390
The point is to get that Yes.&nbsp;

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.640
And you have to get through a lot of No’s
to get that Yes.&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.230
But you have to get the work out there.&nbsp;

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.230
You have to get the work out there!&nbsp;

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.670
It does no good sitting on the shelf.&nbsp;

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:19.840
It does no good sitting on the computer without
anybody seeing it.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.800
Of course you want to scrutinize if the work
is up to par.&nbsp;

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.159
You want to make sure that the work is strong
and you are getting the feedback from people

00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:31.439
who don’t have to be nice to you.&nbsp;

00:03:31.439 --> 00:03:35.090
And that is a really big thing, so it can’t
be your friends who are concerned about maintaining

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:39.290
a friendship who are going to be a little
bit more forgiving because they understand

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.459
your intention.&nbsp;

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:44.019
They know what you went for, you didn’t
quite get there but they saw it enough.

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:47.879
But people who will scrutinize your work and
be demanding of it are telling you “It’s

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:48.879
good.&nbsp;

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:49.879
Get it out there.&nbsp;

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:51.000
Get it out there.”&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.219
That is how you build a career.&nbsp;

00:03:52.219 --> 00:03:55.520
You don’t build a career with one script
that you are precious about.&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:01.370
You build a career writing great script after
great script and getting a fan base that is

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.609
excited to read the next thing.&nbsp;

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:06.150
They know that the next thing is coming and
want to be the first on the list to get it

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:06.151
from you.

